# answered by claude-opus-5-5, prompt /lib/phil/senses f4c9d13865f3, 2026-10-19
sensible	one
perceived_by_sense	two	perceive	P3	the things (houses, rivers) our senses perceive, in the ordinary sense	only what is immediately present to sensory awareness
idea	one
exists_unperceived	one
opinion_contradictory	one
# perceived_by_sense: P2 needs "perceive" in the everyday sense for houses and rivers to count. P3 needs the narrow sense of whatever is directly before the mind, or "only our own ideas" would not follow.
# Each premise is plausible only on its own reading. This is the standard immediate/mediate objection to the passage, so I marked it as two senses, though some readers would say the text means one sense throughout.
